-- title:
--   Points of T_θ are local and reduce to θ
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N \neq 0$ and a set $S$ of natural numbers, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation domain that is complete with respect to its maximal-ideal-adic topology. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N)))$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N$ with $q \notin S$ — to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$, and assume [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N 2`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6), i.e. that the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ spanned by the forms all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers is the whole space. Let $\mathcal{O}'$ be a commutative local ring which is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra whose structure map is a local homomorphism, and let $\psi$ be an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism from $\mathbb{T}_\theta =$ [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131), the localisation of `heckeBaseAlgebra N S 𝒪` at the image under `latticeBaseChange N S 𝒪` of the prime complement of `heckeCharKernel N S 𝒪 θ`, to $\mathcal{O}'$. Then the underlying ring homomorphism of $\psi$ is local, i.e. it carries non-units to non-units, and for every $t$ in the Hecke algebra the residue of $\psi(\pi(t))$ in the residue field of $\mathcal{O}'$, where $\pi$ is [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151), equals the image of $\theta(t)$ under the map of residue fields induced by $\mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{O}'$.
--
--   This is the statement that an arbitrary $\mathcal{O}$-valued (more generally, local-$\mathcal{O}$-algebra-valued) point of the localised Hecke algebra $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ is automatically a local homomorphism and induces the residual eigensystem $\theta$ on Hecke operators. It is used wherever points of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ must be compared with $\theta$ modulo the maximal ideal, for instance in the identification of $\psi \circ \pi$ with the Hecke eigenvalues of a newform and in the ramification statements deduced from such an identification.
